\'s blue, which would make him smart because he\'s blue and green. But wait, he\'s red, so he\'s also green, making him blue, hence smart.\n\nBut earlier, from point 12, all furry things are green, so he\'s green. So he\'s red and green, which makes him blue. So he\'s blue, which is also red. That seems possible.\n\nWait, but if he\'s blue, then from point 16: blue, red things are smart. So he\'s smart. But from point 11: if something is smart and not red, it\'s not green. But Charlie is red, so that rule doesn\'t apply. So he can be green and red and still be smart.\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\nCharlie is young → red.\n\nRed → furry.\n\nFurry → green.\n\nSo Charlie is green.\n\nBut from point 13: if red and green, then blue. So Charlie is blue.\n\nBut being blue and green, from point 15, he\'s smart.\n\nSo Charlie is blue and green, hence smart.\n\nBut the question is whether Charlie is not green. But from point 12, all furry things are green, so Charlie is green.\n\nTherefore, the statement "Charlie is not green" would be false because Charlie is green.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought he\'s blue because of point 13, but that\'s conflicting. Maybe I\'m overcomplicating it.\n\nLet me try a different approach. Let\'s see:.\n\nCharlie is young → red.\n\nRed → furry.\n\nFurry → green.\n\nSo Charlie is green. Therefore, the option is ']] 28%|##8       | 171/600 [13:58<28:20,  3.96s/it]
